A||||11||GT Bastard||Give me your thoughts on 4.10 gearing...||||3||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||08-26-2000||12:19 AM||||Ok, here's the deal.  I'm running 4.10 gears in my tweaked 2.8 behind a 4.10 4sp out of an '84 coupe.  The car accelerates very nicely all the way through 4th gear and it shifts nice and smooth all the time(thanks to 1FST2M6.)  Only problem is that it seems to be pushing the motor too hard on the highway. At 65mph, I'm sitting at about 3,000 RPM.  Even after I get the new motor installed and broken in, I'd rather not run her at a sustained engine speed that high(or higher if I'm driving the speed limit!) when I decide to take a trip somewhere.  The car is autocrossed occasionally and will be run at the track during the summer.  The motor will be pulled in a few weeks, and I've got a healthy Muncie 4sp with 3.65's sitting in my garage. What is everyone's take on the benefits/disadvantages of the two?  Gas mileage doesn't bother me, it's the stress on the motor.  Is it worth the trouble of switching?  The existing tranny shifts like new(nice and crisp.)  The Muncie shifted "ok" when it was in the other car. <P>thanks...<BR>Bastard||||reg||3
Z||000001||Patrick||08-26-2000||04:03 AM||||<BR>Higher RPMs (within reason) do not cause more "stress" on the engine. Lower ratio gears actually make life easier for an engine, especially in stop and go driving. <P>Extended trips on the freeway may be a different matter though on other parts of the drive train. On an old high performance Chevy of mine that had 4:10 gears, I fried the automatic transmission at the Oregon/California border while zooming along I5. It was the middle of summer, and the tranny got so hot from miles and miles of high speed (well over the speed limit) driving that the rear bushing in the tail shaft housing seized onto the drive shaft yoke. Made for a disappointing trip... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Your choice of gearing is going to depend on what kind of driving you normally do- stop and go or freeway driving.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||08-26-2000||07:16 AM||||leave the 4:10 in there and tow it with the subaru on the highway!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  j/k  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||08-26-2000||01:58 PM||||Hmmmm.... How bout synthetic gear oil to keep temps in the tranny down?  1FST, my subaru is 4wd, but it only has like 60hp!  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||08-26-2000||05:02 PM||||I picked the 4.10 for my car for a couple of reasons. My commute to work is pretty much in town driving, so why have a 5 speed? Also, the low gears make it much easier to take to stop and go traffic. It can pull down to 1000 rpm's in 4th and still crawl along with out bucking. Most of the time, I just take off in second gear and then shift to 4th so I only have to shift half as much. <P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
Z||000005||007DOUG||08-26-2000||11:13 PM||||Its been a while since I paid attention, but don't most v6 fieros (depending on stick or atuo tranny) run near 3Krpm doing 70mph?<P>I seem to recall my GX3 automatic is dead on 3K @70mph.<P>Not much diffence between 65-70mph.<P>Now if you're doing 100mph regularly then buy an extended warranty on the rebuild. :-)<P>------------------<BR>4 Indy pace Cars<BR>84 T-top<BR>84 SE<BR>86 SE V6 153K<BR>2 86 GT's<BR>86 GT 3.4 Pearl White T-top<BR>2 88 GT twins<P>However......<BR><I>"Time is my most precious and most perishable of all my possessions."</I>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||08-26-2000||11:20 PM||||got an '84 with the 4:10 tranny but haven't got the car drivable yet (still need a gas tank to start) to try an notice a difference bettween it and my 5 speed izuzu tranny. guess i'll try to pay attention to rpm's at 65 to see what they are compared to what you've posted. if there's not much difference maybe i should look for another 4:10 4 speed for consistency bettween cars.<P>was the 4:10 exclusive to the fiero or would maybe an older citation (or other gm fwd from that period) possibly have one?||||reg||1
